(function (T) { T.unit({ id: "9.3", title: "Cyber security", q: "How are systems attacked, and how do we defend them?", lessons: [
{ t: "Why data is valuable: the CIA triad", o: "I can explain why data needs protecting, using confidentiality, integrity and availability.",
  k: ["cyber security: protecting computers, networks and data from attack, damage or unauthorised access", "confidentiality: only authorised people can see the data", "integrity: the data is accurate and has not been changed without permission"],
  d: ["Name three kinds of data a school holds that would cause harm if they were stolen or changed.", "For example medical records, addresses, exam results, safeguarding notes, bank details."],
  s: ["Why attackers want data|Personal details can be sold or used to commit fraud|Bank and card details steal money directly|Accounts can be used to attack others",
      "The CIA triad|Confidentiality: only the right people can see it|Integrity: it is correct and unchanged|Availability: people who need it can get it when they need it",
      "One attack, different harms|Stolen exam results: confidentiality|Changed grades: integrity|Server knocked offline: availability"],
  a: "Classify the twelve incidents on the sheet by which part of the CIA triad they damage, then rank them by how serious they are for a school and justify your top two. Support: sort the incidents into three columns. Stretch: find an incident that damages all three, and explain how.",
  w: [["What does cyber security mean?", "Protecting computers, networks and data from attack, damage or unauthorised access."], ["What does confidentiality mean?", "Only authorised people can see the data."], ["What does integrity mean?", "The data is accurate and has not been changed without permission."], ["A website is flooded with traffic and nobody can reach it. Which part of the triad is affected?", "Availability."], ["Explain why changing data can be more harmful than stealing it.", "People may make decisions using wrong data without knowing it has been changed, for example a wrong dose in a medical record."]],
  x: ["Name the three parts of the CIA triad.", "Confidentiality, integrity, availability."] },
{ t: "Malware", o: "I can describe the main types of malware and how each spreads.",
  k: ["malware: malicious software designed to damage, disrupt or gain unauthorised access", "virus: malware that attaches to a file and spreads when the file is run or shared", "ransomware: malware that encrypts a victim's files and demands payment for the key"],
  d: ["What do you think the word 'malware' is short for?", "Malicious software."],
  s: ["Spreading|Virus: attaches to a file; needs a person to run or share it|Worm: copies itself across a network on its own|Trojan horse: pretends to be useful software so the user installs it",
      "What it does|Spyware: secretly records activity such as key presses and sends it to the attacker|Adware: shows unwanted adverts|Ransomware: encrypts files and demands payment",
      "Defences|Anti-malware software, kept up to date|Install updates to close security holes|Only download from trusted sources|Keep backups: the best answer to ransomware"],
  a: "Complete the malware fact file: for each type, how it spreads, what it does and the best defence. Then match six news-style descriptions to the type of malware. Support: use the definition cards. Stretch: explain why a worm can spread faster than a virus.",
  w: [["What is malware?", "Malicious software designed to damage, disrupt or gain unauthorised access."], ["How does a worm differ from a virus?", "A worm spreads across a network by itself; a virus needs a person to run or share an infected file."], ["What does spyware do?", "It secretly records activity, such as key presses, and sends it to the attacker."], ["Why is a recent backup the best defence against ransomware?", "Files can be restored from the backup without paying."], ["Explain how a trojan horse tricks a user.", "It pretends to be useful or harmless software, so the user installs it themselves."]],
  x: ["Which malware secretly records the keys you press?", "Spyware (a keylogger)."] },
{ t: "Lab: case-study carousel", o: "I can analyse real-style incidents and identify the attack, the harm and the missing defence.",
  k: ["incident: an event in which security was broken", "vulnerability: a weakness that an attacker can use", "mitigation: an action that reduces risk"],
  d: ["A company was attacked through a computer that had not been updated for two years. What was the vulnerability?", "Missing security updates."],
  s: ["The carousel|Six fictional case studies around the room|Eight minutes at each|Complete the analysis grid",
      "For each case|What happened? What type of attack was it?|Which part of the CIA triad was harmed?|What vulnerability was used?",
      "Mitigation|What one change would have stopped it?|What would have reduced the damage?|Who was responsible for that change?"],
  a: "Visit each station and complete the analysis grid. Then choose the case where the mitigation was cheapest and write a short recommendation to that organisation. Support: complete four stations with the prompt cards. Stretch: write a seventh case study for another group to analyse.",
  w: [["What is a vulnerability?", "A weakness that an attacker can use."], ["What is mitigation?", "An action that reduces risk."], ["Give one vulnerability from the case studies and its mitigation.", "For example missing updates, mitigated by installing updates automatically."], ["Which part of the CIA triad was harmed in the hospital case?", "Availability (systems were locked), and possibly confidentiality."], ["Explain why people are often the weakest part of a security system.", "Attackers can trick people into giving access, and people make mistakes, reuse passwords or skip updates."]],
  x: ["Which case study surprised you most, and why?", "A named case with a reason."] },
{ t: "Social engineering and phishing", o: "I can explain how attackers manipulate people and how to resist.",
  k: ["social engineering: manipulating people into giving away information or access", "phishing: fake messages that trick people into revealing data or clicking a harmful link", "pretexting: inventing a believable story to gain someone's trust"],
  d: ["Someone phones the school office, says they are from IT support and asks for a teacher's password to fix a problem. What should happen?", "Refuse. Real IT staff never ask for passwords. Report the call."],
  s: ["Social engineering|Attacks the person, not the computer|Uses trust, fear, urgency or helpfulness|Phone calls, emails, messages and in person",
      "Common methods|Phishing: fake emails and messages with links|Pretexting: 'I'm from IT and need your password'|Shoulder surfing: watching someone type a PIN|Baiting: leaving an infected USB stick to be found",
      "Resisting|Never share passwords, whoever asks|Check by contacting the organisation another way|Slow down when a message creates urgency|Report it"],
  a: "Identify the social engineering method in each of the eight scenarios and write the safe response. Then annotate a fictional phishing email with every warning sign. Support: choose from the method cards. Stretch: explain why phishing aimed at one named person (spear phishing) is more successful.",
  w: [["What is social engineering?", "Manipulating people into giving away information or access."], ["Give three warning signs of a phishing email.", "Any three: urgency, generic greeting, mismatched sender address, unexpected link or attachment, spelling errors, request for personal details."], ["What is shoulder surfing?", "Watching someone enter a password or PIN."], ["An email asks you to confirm your password by clicking a link. What should you do?", "Do not click. Go to the real site yourself or contact the organisation, and report the email."], ["Explain why creating urgency helps an attacker.", "People act quickly without thinking or checking when they feel under pressure."]],
  x: ["Why should you never give your password to someone claiming to be IT support?", "Real IT staff do not need it, so the request is a sign of an attack."] },
{ t: "Pharming, brute force and denial of service", o: "I can describe pharming, brute-force and denial-of-service attacks and how to prevent them.",
  k: ["pharming: redirecting users from a real website to a fake one without them knowing", "brute-force attack: trying every possible password until one works", "denial of service (DoS): flooding a server with requests so it cannot serve real users"],
  d: ["How many different 4-digit PINs are there?", "10 000."],
  s: ["Pharming|Malicious code on the computer, or a changed DNS record, sends the user to a fake site|The address bar may even look right|Defences: anti-malware, checking the certificate and the https padlock",
      "Brute force|A program tries every combination|4-digit PIN: 10 000 tries. 8 small letters: about 209 billion|Defences: long passwords, a mix of characters, locking after a few wrong attempts",
      "Denial of service|Huge numbers of requests overload the server|Distributed (DDoS): many infected computers attack at once|Defences: a firewall and services that filter the traffic"],
  a: "Calculate how many combinations brute force must try for six password rules, then match three incident reports to the attack type and write a defence for each. Support: complete the combinations table for digits and small letters only. Stretch: if a program tries one billion passwords a second, how long would each password take in the worst case?",
  w: [["What is pharming?", "Redirecting users from a real website to a fake one without them knowing."], ["How does a brute-force attack work?", "A program tries every possible password until one works."], ["How many combinations does an 8-letter password of small letters have?", "26 to the power 8, which is 208 827 064 576."], ["Give two defences against brute-force attacks.", "Any two: long passwords, a mix of character types, locking the account after a few wrong attempts, two-factor authentication."], ["Explain why a distributed denial of service is harder to stop than one from a single computer.", "The traffic comes from many different computers, so blocking one address does not stop it."]],
  x: ["Which attack aims to damage availability?", "Denial of service."] },
{ t: "Lab: design an anti-phishing campaign", o: "I can communicate security advice clearly to a particular audience.",
  k: ["campaign: a planned set of messages to change behaviour", "audience: the people the message is for", "call to action: the one thing you want people to do"],
  d: ["Which is more likely to change behaviour: a long policy document, or a short, clear poster with one rule?", "The short, clear poster."],
  s: ["The brief|Design a campaign for the staff of a busy school office|Goal: fewer staff click phishing links|Three items: a poster, an email and a two-minute talk",
      "Make it work|One clear call to action: 'Stop, check, report'|Real-looking (fictional) examples of warning signs|Say exactly how to report",
      "Measure it|How would you know the campaign worked?|Suggest a safe test, such as a practice phishing email|Report how many staff reported it"],
  a: "In groups, create the poster, the email and the outline of the talk. Present to another group and collect one piece of feedback. Support: use the poster template. Stretch: design a simple way to measure whether the campaign worked.",
  w: [["What is a call to action?", "The one thing you want people to do."], ["Write your campaign's call to action.", "A short, clear instruction."], ["Who is your audience, and how did that affect your design?", "The audience and a design decision linked to it."], ["List three warning signs your campaign teaches.", "Any three warning signs of phishing."], ["Explain how an organisation could measure whether staff are better at spotting phishing.", "Send a safe practice phishing email before and after the campaign and compare how many staff report it or click."]],
  x: ["What feedback did the other group give you?", "A specific comment."] },
{ t: "Authentication: passwords, biometrics and two-step", o: "I can compare methods of authentication.",
  k: ["authentication: checking that a user is who they claim to be", "biometrics: authentication using a body feature, such as a fingerprint or face", "two-step verification: requiring a second proof, such as a code sent to a phone"],
  d: ["Name three ways you have unlocked a device or account.", "For example a password, a PIN, a fingerprint, a face scan, a code sent to a phone."],
  s: ["Three factors|Something you know: password, PIN|Something you have: phone, card, security key|Something you are: fingerprint, face, voice",
      "Comparing|Passwords: cheap, but can be guessed, stolen or reused|Biometrics: quick and hard to share, but cannot be changed if copied|Codes on a phone: need the phone as well as the password",
      "Two-step verification|Combines two different factors|A stolen password alone is not enough|Turn it on for email: email can reset other accounts"],
  a: "Complete the comparison table for passwords, biometrics and two-step verification (security, convenience, cost, what happens if it is stolen). Recommend authentication for four systems. Support: complete the table with the statements provided. Stretch: explain why a password plus a security question is not two-factor authentication.",
  w: [["What is authentication?", "Checking that a user is who they claim to be."], ["Give one advantage and one disadvantage of biometrics.", "Advantage: quick, hard to share or forget. Disadvantage: cannot be changed if copied, or can fail for some users."], ["What is two-step verification?", "Requiring a second proof, such as a code sent to a phone, as well as a password."], ["Recommend authentication for an online bank account and justify it.", "A strong password with two-step verification, because a stolen password alone would then not give access to money."], ["Explain why it matters most to protect an email account.", "It can be used to reset the passwords of other accounts."]],
  x: ["Which factor is a fingerprint: know, have or are?", "Are."] },
{ t: "Firewalls, access levels, updates and backups", o: "I can describe technical defences and what each protects against.",
  k: ["firewall: hardware or software that monitors traffic and blocks anything not allowed by its rules", "access level: what a particular user is allowed to see or change", "backup: a copy of data kept so it can be restored"],
  d: ["Should every member of school staff be able to see every student's medical record? Why?", "No. Only people who need it, such as the nurse. This is an access level."],
  s: ["Firewall|Sits between the network and the internet|Checks traffic against rules: allow or block|Blocks unauthorised access and some malware",
      "Access levels and updates|Give each user only the access their job needs|Read-only, read and write, or none|Updates fix security holes: install them promptly",
      "Backups|Copy data regularly to a separate place|Keep one copy off-site or offline|Test that a backup can actually be restored"],
  a: "Match each defence to the threats it protects against, then set access levels for six staff roles in a school database. Support: use the defence cards. Stretch: write a backup plan for a small business stating what, how often, where and how it is tested.",
  w: [["What does a firewall do?", "It monitors traffic and blocks anything not allowed by its rules."], ["What is an access level?", "What a particular user is allowed to see or change."], ["Why should security updates be installed promptly?", "They fix weaknesses that attackers already know about."], ["Why should one backup be kept off-site or offline?", "A fire, theft or ransomware attack could destroy the original and an attached backup together."], ["Explain how access levels limit the damage if one account is taken over.", "The attacker can only reach what that account was allowed to reach."]],
  x: ["Which defence protects against data loss from a fire?", "An off-site backup."] },
{ t: "Lab: security audit of a fictional business", o: "I can audit an organisation's security and prioritise improvements.",
  k: ["audit: a careful check against a set of standards", "risk: the chance of harm multiplied by how serious it would be", "priority: what should be done first"],
  d: ["A shop has 20 problems and money to fix three. How should it choose?", "Fix the ones with the highest risk: most likely and most harmful."],
  s: ["The business|A fictional dental clinic with five staff|Read its IT description and the interview notes|Every detail may matter",
      "Audit|Use the 15-point checklist|Record each problem and the threat it allows|Rate likelihood and impact from 1 to 3",
      "Recommend|Risk = likelihood x impact|Choose the top three by risk|Give each a specific action and who should do it"],
  a: "Complete the audit, calculate risk scores and write a one-page report with three prioritised recommendations. Support: audit the first eight checklist points. Stretch: estimate the cost of each recommendation and justify spending on the top one.",
  w: [["What is a security audit?", "A careful check of security against a set of standards."], ["How is a risk score calculated in this task?", "Likelihood multiplied by impact."], ["What was your highest-risk problem?", "A problem from the audit with its score."], ["Write one recommendation as a specific action.", "For example: switch on automatic updates on all five computers this week."], ["Explain why the cheapest fix is not always the first priority.", "Priority depends on risk. A cheap fix to a low risk can wait while an expensive fix to a high risk cannot."]],
  x: ["What were your top three recommendations?", "Three actions."] },
{ t: "Ciphers through history", o: "I can encrypt and decrypt with a Caesar cipher and explain how it can be broken.",
  k: ["encryption: scrambling data so only someone with the key can read it", "plaintext: the original readable message", "ciphertext: the scrambled message"],
  d: ["Shift every letter of CAT forward by 1. What do you get?", "DBU."],
  s: ["The Caesar cipher|Shift every letter a fixed number of places|Shift 3: A becomes D, B becomes E|HELLO becomes KHOOR",
      "Decrypting|Shift back by the same number|The shift is the key|Sender and receiver must both know it",
      "Breaking it|Only 25 possible shifts: try them all (brute force)|Or count letters: E is the most common in English|Modern ciphers have far too many keys to try"],
  a: "Encrypt and decrypt messages with a cipher wheel, then break three messages without the key using brute force or letter frequency. Check your work with Networks, then Encryption, in CS Toolkit. Support: use a shift of 3 throughout. Stretch: break the message using letter frequency only and explain your reasoning.",
  w: [["What is encryption?", "Scrambling data so only someone with the key can read it."], ["Encrypt ATTACK with a shift of 3.", "DWWDFN."], ["Decrypt FDW, which used a shift of 3.", "CAT."], ["How many different keys does a Caesar cipher have?", "25 useful shifts."], ["Explain how letter frequency helps to break a Caesar cipher.", "The most common letter in the ciphertext probably stands for E, which reveals the shift."]],
  x: ["What is the difference between plaintext and ciphertext?", "Plaintext is readable; ciphertext is the encrypted version."] },
{ t: "Symmetric and asymmetric encryption", o: "I can explain symmetric and asymmetric encryption.",
  k: ["symmetric encryption: the same key is used to encrypt and decrypt", "asymmetric encryption: a public key encrypts and a matching private key decrypts", "key: the value that controls how data is encrypted and decrypted"],
  d: ["You want to send a friend a secret message, but you have never met to agree a key. What is the problem?", "Any key you send could be intercepted."],
  s: ["Symmetric|One shared key, like the Caesar shift|Fast|Problem: the key must be shared safely first",
      "Asymmetric|Each person has a key pair: public and private|Anyone can encrypt with your public key|Only your private key can decrypt it, and it is never sent",
      "In practice|HTTPS uses asymmetric encryption to agree a secret key safely|Then symmetric encryption for the rest, because it is faster|Keys are hundreds or thousands of bits long"],
  a: "Use the Encryption tab of the Networks page in CS Toolkit to encrypt a number with a public key and decrypt it with the private key. Complete the comparison table and the padlock-and-key analogy. Support: follow the worked example with p = 17 and q = 23. Stretch: explain why knowing the public key does not reveal the private key when the primes are huge.",
  w: [["What is symmetric encryption?", "The same key is used to encrypt and decrypt."], ["What is the main problem with symmetric encryption?", "The key must be shared safely before messages can be sent."], ["In asymmetric encryption, which key is used to encrypt a message to you?", "Your public key."], ["Which key must never be shared?", "The private key."], ["Explain why HTTPS uses both kinds of encryption.", "Asymmetric encryption lets the browser and server agree a secret key without sending it openly. Symmetric encryption is then used because it is faster."]],
  x: ["In the simulator with p = 17 and q = 23, what is n?", "391."] },
{ t: "Lab: encrypt, exchange and break messages", o: "I can use and compare encryption methods in practice.",
  k: ["interception: capturing data as it travels", "key exchange: agreeing a key between two parties", "secure channel: a connection that eavesdroppers cannot read"],
  d: ["Decrypt KHOOR with a shift of 3.", "HELLO."],
  s: ["Round 1: symmetric|Agree a Caesar shift with your partner in secret|Exchange encrypted notes through a 'postman'|The postman tries to read them",
      "Round 2: no shared key|You cannot meet to agree a key|Use the simulator: send your partner your public key|They encrypt a number with it; only you can decrypt",
      "Compare|What could the postman learn in each round?|Which round needed a meeting first?|Which would you trust for bank details?"],
  a: "Play both rounds in groups of three (sender, receiver, postman), record what the postman could learn, and swap roles. Write a comparison of the two rounds. Support: play round 1 only, with a shift card. Stretch: as the postman in round 1, break the message without the key.",
  w: [["What is interception?", "Capturing data as it travels."], ["In round 1, what did the postman need to read the messages?", "The shift (the key), or time to try all 25 shifts."], ["In round 2, what did the postman see?", "The public key and an encrypted number, which could not be decrypted without the private key."], ["Which round needed a secret meeting first?", "Round 1 (symmetric)."], ["Explain why a Caesar cipher would not protect bank details.", "There are only 25 keys, so an attacker can try them all almost instantly."]],
  x: ["Which method solved the key exchange problem?", "Asymmetric encryption."] },
{ t: "Errors in transmission: parity bits", o: "I can explain how a parity bit detects an error.",
  k: ["transmission error: a bit changed while data is being sent", "parity bit: an extra bit added so that the number of 1s is always even (or always odd)", "parity check: counting the 1s on arrival to detect an error"],
  d: ["Electrical interference flips one bit of a byte during transmission. Would the receiver know?", "Not unless some extra checking information is sent."],
  s: ["Why errors happen|Interference, a damaged cable, a weak wireless signal|A 0 becomes a 1, or a 1 becomes a 0|The receiver needs a way to notice",
      "Even parity|Count the 1s in the 7 data bits|Add a parity bit so the total number of 1s is even|1010011 has four 1s, so the parity bit is 0",
      "Checking|The receiver counts the 1s|An odd count means an error|If two bits flip, the count is still even: the error is missed"],
  a: "Add even and odd parity bits to ten 7-bit codes, then check ten received bytes and identify which have errors. Support: use the counting grid. Stretch: find a two-bit error that a parity check misses, and explain why.",
  w: [["What is a parity bit?", "An extra bit added so that the number of 1s is always even (or always odd)."], ["Using even parity, what is the parity bit for 1100001?", "1 (there are three 1s)."], ["Using even parity, which byte has an error: 10110100 or 01101101?", "01101101 (five 1s, an odd number)."], ["Using odd parity, what is the parity bit for 0110110?", "1 (there are four 1s)."], ["Explain why a parity check cannot detect an error where two bits have changed.", "Two flips keep the number of 1s even (or odd), so the count still looks correct."]],
  x: ["Can a single parity bit tell you which bit is wrong?", "No, only that an error has happened."] },
{ t: "Parity blocks: locating the error", o: "I can use a parity block to locate and correct a single-bit error.",
  k: ["parity block: several bytes sent together with a parity bit on each row and a parity byte for the columns", "parity byte: an extra byte holding the parity of each column", "correct: to fix an error without resending"],
  d: ["A parity bit says a byte is wrong but not which bit. How could you find the exact bit?", "Also check each column, and see where a failing row and a failing column cross."],
  s: ["Building a block|Each row: 7 data bits and a parity bit|After the rows: a parity byte, one parity bit for each column|Even parity is used throughout",
      "Finding the error|Check every row and every column|One row fails and one column fails|The error is where they cross: flip that bit",
      "Limits|Two errors in one row: the row passes but two columns fail|Then the error is detected but cannot be located|The receiver asks for the data to be sent again"],
  a: "Build a parity block for three 7-bit codes, then find and correct the error in four received blocks. Check each in the Parity check tab of the Networks page in CS Toolkit. Support: build the block with the row parity bits given. Stretch: corrupt two bits so the error is detected but cannot be located, and explain why.",
  w: [["What is a parity byte?", "An extra byte holding the parity bit for each column."], ["Rows 1001101, 0110001 and 1110010 use even parity with the parity bit at the end. Write each row with its parity bit.", "10011010, 01100011, 11100100."], ["Give the parity byte for that block.", "00011101."], ["Row 3 and column 6 fail the parity check. What does the receiver do?", "Flips the bit at row 3, column 6 to correct it."], ["Explain why a parity block can correct a single error but a single parity bit cannot.", "The failing row and failing column together give the exact position; a single parity bit only says the byte is wrong."]],
  x: ["Where is the error in a parity block?", "Where the failing row and the failing column cross."] },
{ t: "Lab: corrupt-a-bit investigation", o: "I can investigate when parity checks work and when they fail.",
  k: ["investigation: testing ideas and recording results to find patterns", "detected: noticed by the check", "undetected: missed by the check"],
  d: ["Predict: if you flip two bits in the same row of a parity block, what will the checks show?", "The row passes, but two columns fail."],
  s: ["Method|Open the Parity check tab in CS Toolkit|Flip one bit, then two, then three, in different places|Record which rows and columns fail",
      "Record|Number of bits flipped and where|Detected? Located? Corrected correctly?|Take a screenshot of an interesting case",
      "Conclude|When does a parity block correct an error?|When does it only detect one?|Can you make four flips go completely undetected?"],
  a: "Run at least ten trials and complete the results table. Write three conclusions, including one case where errors were missed. Support: run the six trials listed on the sheet. Stretch: find the smallest number of flips that goes completely undetected and explain the pattern.",
  w: [["What happened when you flipped one bit?", "One row and one column failed, so the error was located and could be corrected."], ["What happened when you flipped two bits in the same row?", "The row passed but two columns failed: detected, not located."], ["Describe a case where the errors went undetected.", "Four bits at the corners of a rectangle: every row and column still has even parity."], ["Why does the receiver ask for the data again when it cannot locate an error?", "It knows the data is wrong but not how to fix it."], ["Explain why other methods, such as checksums and automatic repeat requests, are also used.", "Parity misses some errors, so further checks and resending make transmission more reliable."]],
  x: ["Write your most important conclusion.", "A conclusion supported by results."] },
{ t: "Law, ethics and careers in security", o: "I can discuss the law and ethics of hacking and describe careers in cyber security.",
  k: ["unauthorised access: using a computer system without permission", "ethical hacker: a security expert paid to find weaknesses with permission", "penetration test: an authorised simulated attack to find weaknesses"],
  d: ["Is it acceptable to log in to a friend's account 'as a joke' if you know their password? Why?", "No. It is unauthorised access and is illegal in most countries, even as a joke."],
  s: ["The law|Most countries make unauthorised access a crime|So are changing data without permission and spreading malware|Check the computer misuse law where you live",
      "Ethics|Permission is the difference between an attack and a test|Ethical hackers sign an agreement before testing|Responsible disclosure: report a weakness privately and give time to fix it",
      "Careers|Penetration tester, security analyst, incident responder|Digital forensics, security engineer|Skills: networking, programming, problem-solving, honesty"],
  a: "Discuss the six ethical dilemmas in groups and record a decision with reasons for each. Then research one security career: what the job involves, the skills needed and a route into it. Support: use the dilemma cards with guiding questions. Stretch: write a short code of conduct for a school cyber club.",
  w: [["What is unauthorised access?", "Using a computer system without permission."], ["What is an ethical hacker?", "A security expert paid to find weaknesses with permission."], ["What is a penetration test?", "An authorised simulated attack to find weaknesses."], ["You find a security hole in the school website. What is the responsible thing to do?", "Report it privately to the school without using or sharing it."], ["Explain why the same action can be legal for an ethical hacker and illegal for someone else.", "The ethical hacker has written permission from the owner; without permission the access is unauthorised."]],
  x: ["Name one cyber security career and one skill it needs.", "For example a penetration tester, who needs networking skills."] },
{ t: "Assessed test and extended answer", o: "I can show what I know about cyber security in a test.",
  k: ["extended answer: a longer answer with linked points in a logical order", "recommend: to suggest the best action, with reasons", "justify: to give reasons for a choice"],
  d: ["A six-mark question asks you to recommend defences. What must each point include?", "A defence and why it protects against a specific threat."],
  s: ["The test|Definitions of threats and defences|A parity question|A six-mark answer recommending defences for an organisation",
      "Extended answer|Name a threat that applies to the organisation|Recommend a defence|Explain how it reduces that threat|Repeat for two or three threats",
      "Check|Have you used the organisation's details?|Is every defence matched to a threat?|Is the language precise?"],
  a: "Complete the test under test conditions, including the six-mark question. Support: a key-word bank is provided for the extended answer. Stretch: attempt the extension question on why HTTPS uses both kinds of encryption.",
  w: [["Define phishing.", "Fake messages that trick people into revealing data or clicking a harmful link."], ["Describe how a firewall protects a network.", "It checks traffic against rules and blocks anything not allowed, stopping unauthorised access."], ["Using even parity, which byte is wrong: 11010010 or 11100011?", "11100011 (five 1s)."], ["Give one difference between symmetric and asymmetric encryption.", "Symmetric uses one shared key; asymmetric uses a public key to encrypt and a private key to decrypt."], ["A small online shop stores customers' card details. Recommend two defences and justify them.", "For example encryption, so stolen data cannot be read, and two-step verification for staff logins, so stolen passwords are not enough. Accept other justified pairs."]],
  x: ["How many threat-and-defence pairs did your extended answer contain?", "A number, ideally 3."] },
{ t: "Feedback and reflection", o: "I can improve my answers and reflect on my learning.",
  k: ["feedback: comments that help you improve", "redraft: to write an improved version", "target: a specific thing to work on next"],
  d: ["Underline every threat and every defence in your extended answer. Is each defence matched to a threat?", "A self-check."],
  s: ["Read your feedback|Which question types cost marks?|Were threats and defences matched?|Ask about anything unclear",
      "Redraft|Add any missing threat-and-defence pairs|Use precise key words|Link each point to the organisation",
